<Base
  title="About — Tan Lines"
  description="Why Tan Lines covers Melanotan II as history and analysis, the editorial rules it follows, and what it will never publish."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About Tan Lines</h1>
    <p>
      If you searched for Melanotan II, you probably found two kinds of pages:
      sites selling unregulated vials, and thin warnings telling you nothing.
      Tan Lines is the third thing &mdash; the actual story. The Record is the
      file we keep on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This domain used to sell skincare. It doesn&rsquo;t anymore. Tan Lines
      publishes here now.
    </p>
    <p>
      Melanotan II is an unapproved compound with a genuinely remarkable
      history: born from legitimate melanoma-prevention research, escaped into
      a global gray market, and ancestor to two FDA-approved medicines that
      have nothing to do with tanning. We cover that history the way a science
      journalist would &mdash; including the harms, the hype, and the open
      scientific question underneath it all.
    </p>
    <h2>The editorial rules</h2>
    <p>
      We do not sell Melanotan II or anything else on this domain. We do not
      publish sourcing information, dosing guidance, or usage advice for
      unapproved compounds &mdash; not framed as harm reduction, not framed as
      history. The cautionary tale is the point, and it stays a tale.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every article is dated, grounded in published research and public
      records, and honest about uncertainty. When the evidence is thin, the
      article says so. When popular claims are wrong, the article says that
      too &mdash; including claims made by people selling things.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full method &mdash; how we source, how we handle corrections, and what
      we will never publish &mdash; is set out in our
      <a href="/standards/">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Where we&rsquo;re openly opinionated</h2>
    <p>
      We think the world still needs what the original researchers were
      chasing: a rigorously tested, approved medicine that gives human skin
      durable protection from ultraviolet damage, delivered in a form ordinary
      people would actually use. We call that position what it is &mdash; hope,
      clearly labeled &mdash; and we analyze what is standing between here and
      there. Nothing on this site is medical advice.
    </p>
  </article>
  <style>
    article { padding-top: 3rem; }
    h1 { font-size: 2.2rem; }
  </style>
</Base>
